  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Now I may have information wrong here, because docs are slightly unclear (fix that too), but here's what I know and what I believe should be reworked:\n\n1. Uploaded worlds are private by default.\n2. Worlds must be approved to become public.\n3. Approval can take anywhere from an hour to 2+ days to be approved (and 12-24 hours more for approval to be reflected in the game).\n4. Public worlds that are updated immediately become private.\n5. Updated worlds that wish to become public again must re-submit for approval.\n\nBased on this, I have inferred that a creator who wants to update their world will experience anywhere from 12 hours to 3 or more days of complete downtime, during which their world is private and undiscoverable.\n\nI'd like to see improvements to this system in these areas:\n\n1. Differentiate between world versions. The latest public version is the one shown. All else is invisible.\n2. A new version should of course still be private by default, but should not overwrite the latest public version.\n3. Differentiate between \"approved for public\" and \"is public\". This adds convenience for my fourth point.\n4. For those that wish to purposefully take their world private again, add a checkbox on the site. A version of a world that is approved and later taken private by the creator should be allowed to go public again without re-approval.\n5. The approval form should be part of the upload process in unity.\n\nCombined, these 5 points add a fair amount of technical challenge, I know, but I also know AWS S3 supports versioning of files, which may make this easier. And the guesswork of making worlds public will largely disappear and make world making much more enticing and the rules much more clear.",
